1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,100
At 12:45 in the afternoon of
Friday, 18 September 1970,

2
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,940
a doctor at St Mary Abbot's Hospital
in London pronounced a patient dead.

3
00:00:18,484 --> 00:00:20,169
The patient was Jimi Hendrix.

4
00:00:20,469 --> 00:00:21,729
He was twenty-seven years old.

5
00:00:22,227 --> 00:00:27,127
He had reached the hospital an hour earlier,
and nobody there knew who he was until his

6
00:00:27,127 --> 00:00:29,067
tour manager came to identify him.

7
00:00:29,577 --> 00:00:34,617
This is the story of his last weeks, and of what
the official record says about how they ended.

8
00:00:35,176 --> 00:00:39,741
Born in Seattle in 1942, he had
become famous in London in 1966,

9
00:00:40,561 --> 00:00:42,781
and he made three albums
in less than two years.

10
00:00:43,489 --> 00:00:47,869
A year before his death, at Woodstock, he
closed the festival by playing the American

11
00:00:47,869 --> 00:00:52,089
national anthem on an electric guitar,
and people argued about it for weeks.

12
00:00:52,811 --> 00:00:55,631
Asked on television what the
fuss was about, he shrugged,

13
00:00:55,971 --> 00:00:58,011
and said he was an American,
so he had played it.

14
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:12,747
By the summer of 1970 he was among
the most famous musicians alive,

15
00:01:13,267 --> 00:01:14,487
and one of the most tired.

16
00:01:15,072 --> 00:01:19,297
His new recording studio, Electric
Lady, was taking shape in New York,

17
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:22,377
and he wanted to stay there
and finish his next album.

18
00:01:22,993 --> 00:01:25,833
But a tour of Europe was booked,
and the bills had to be paid.

19
00:01:26,453 --> 00:01:30,053
His bass player, Billy Cox, remembered
how Hendrix felt about going.

20
00:01:58,895 --> 00:02:02,840
In the last week of August he crossed the
Atlantic to play the Isle of Wight Festival,

21
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:07,080
off the south coast of England, one
of the largest festivals ever held.

22
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:12,008
Estimates for the whole festival run from six
hundred thousand to seven hundred thousand

23
00:02:12,008 --> 00:02:15,208
people, and the performances
were professionally filmed.

24
00:02:15,886 --> 00:02:18,746
Hendrix did not go on until
the early hours of Monday,

25
00:02:19,106 --> 00:02:23,186
31 August, with Mitch Mitchell
on drums and Billy Cox on bass,

26
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:25,846
and the set was dogged
by technical problems.

27
00:02:26,451 --> 00:02:29,776
Yet the film that survives
shows a band in bright colours,

28
00:02:30,136 --> 00:02:32,756
playing to a crowd that had
waited late into the night.

29
00:02:50,662 --> 00:02:56,247
Two days later, in Aarhus, in Denmark, he
stopped a concert after only three songs,

30
00:02:56,807 --> 00:03:00,547
and is reported to have told the audience
that he had been dead a long time.

31
00:03:01,101 --> 00:03:05,906
On Saturday, 5 September, he was due to
headline a small festival on the German island

32
00:03:05,906 --> 00:03:08,186
of Fehmarn, called Love and Peace.

33
00:03:08,849 --> 00:03:13,609
A gale and heavy rain blew in, and his tour
manager would not let him play on a stage

34
00:03:13,609 --> 00:03:14,249
in that weather.

35
00:03:14,858 --> 00:03:17,638
Hells Angels bikers on the
site took over security,

36
00:03:17,998 --> 00:03:19,638
and there was trouble
throughout the weekend.

37
00:03:20,254 --> 00:03:24,094
The next day, at about one in the
afternoon, the band went on in the cold,

38
00:03:24,314 --> 00:03:26,074
in front of about thirty thousand people.

39
00:03:45,038 --> 00:03:48,053
Some of the crowd booed.
Hendrix answered with a joke,

40
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:49,053
and played on.

41
00:03:49,695 --> 00:03:53,675
The set ended with Voodoo Child, and
Hendrix left the island by helicopter.

42
00:03:54,417 --> 00:03:56,622
It was the last full concert he ever gave.

43
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:12,187
Three days later the tour was cancelled.

44
00:04:12,907 --> 00:04:15,967
Billy Cox, who had been unwell,
flew home to the United States.

45
00:04:16,622 --> 00:04:20,497
Hendrix went back to London, where he had
first become famous four years earlier,

46
00:04:20,877 --> 00:04:22,757
and took a room at the Cumberland Hotel.

47
00:04:23,377 --> 00:04:25,477
An interview with the
journalist Keith Altham,

48
00:04:25,677 --> 00:04:29,137
dated 11 September, is
often presented as his last.

49
00:04:29,803 --> 00:04:32,608
In it he explained why he
had smashed guitars on stage,

50
00:04:33,108 --> 00:04:35,328
and why he thought of
the stage as his room.

51
00:04:45,662 --> 00:04:47,087
He ended on a lighter note.

52
00:04:57,833 --> 00:05:03,393
On Wednesday, 16 September, he went to Ronnie
Scott's club in Soho to watch his friend

53
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:05,273
Eric Burdon play with the band War.

54
00:05:05,853 --> 00:05:10,058
Between sets he asked if he could join them,
and he played a couple of songs with them

55
00:05:10,058 --> 00:05:10,898
as the night ended.

56
00:05:11,411 --> 00:05:13,636
It was the last time he played in public.

57
00:05:14,456 --> 00:05:17,156
What survives is an
audience tape, not a film.

58
00:05:17,704 --> 00:05:22,604
The next afternoon, Thursday, his former
girlfriend Kathy Etchingham saw him in Kensington

59
00:05:22,604 --> 00:05:24,304
Market, and he seemed well.

60
00:05:40,067 --> 00:05:43,187
By Monika Dannemann's account, he
spent the evening with friends,

61
00:05:43,607 --> 00:05:48,127
and later the two of them had a meal and
wine at her flat in the Samarkand Hotel,

62
00:05:48,527 --> 00:05:49,187
in Notting Hill.

63
00:05:49,705 --> 00:05:52,510
Dannemann, a German figure
skater, was his girlfriend.

64
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:57,150
She says he went to a party after midnight, and
they came home at about three in the morning.

65
00:05:57,727 --> 00:06:00,572
They talked until about
seven, and then went to sleep.

66
00:06:06,067 --> 00:06:08,887
Here the record begins, and
here it gets complicated.

67
00:06:09,596 --> 00:06:12,036
Dannemann called an ambulance
at 11:18 in the morning,

68
00:06:12,296 --> 00:06:14,320
and the crew arrived at 11:27.

69
00:06:14,765 --> 00:06:17,710
They found Hendrix alone in
the bedroom, unresponsive.

70
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:20,710
Dannemann said she rode
with him to the hospital,

71
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:22,790
and the crew later said she did not.

72
00:06:23,387 --> 00:06:26,092
At St Mary Abbot's, doctors
tried to revive him,

73
00:06:26,352 --> 00:06:28,332
and he was pronounced dead at 12:45.

74
00:06:28,938 --> 00:06:33,938
The post-mortem was carried out on 21
September by the forensic pathologist Professor

75
00:06:33,938 --> 00:06:35,118
Robert Donald Teare.

76
00:06:35,773 --> 00:06:39,113
Its finding was that Hendrix
had died from inhaling vomit,

77
00:06:39,353 --> 00:06:42,893
after intoxication with barbiturates,
a type of sleeping tablet.

78
00:06:43,422 --> 00:06:47,082
Dannemann said he had taken nine
of her prescribed Vesparax tablets.

79
00:06:47,562 --> 00:06:49,762
The recommended dose was half of one.

80
00:06:50,313 --> 00:06:55,633
The inquest opened on 23 September
and ended on 28 September 1970.

81
00:06:56,273 --> 00:07:01,578
The coroner, Gavin Thurston, found no evidence
that Hendrix had meant to take his own life.

82
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:06,660
But he recorded an open verdict, because there
was insufficient evidence of how the circumstances

83
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:07,420
came about.

84
00:07:07,983 --> 00:07:10,528
An open verdict is not
a finding of foul play.

85
00:07:11,088 --> 00:07:13,828
It means the court could not say
more than the evidence allowed.

86
00:07:14,437 --> 00:07:17,202
Over the years, Dannemann's
statements differed on details,

87
00:07:17,562 --> 00:07:20,802
such as what time she woke, and
how many tablets were missing.

88
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:25,393
In 1992, Etchingham asked the
authorities to reopen the inquest.

89
00:07:26,053 --> 00:07:30,873
Scotland Yard looked again, and in 1993
the Attorney General decided not to.

90
00:07:31,432 --> 00:07:34,612
A Home Office file from that
year, titled Coroners cases:

91
00:07:34,612 --> 00:07:38,692
death of Jimi Hendrix, is held
at The National Archives in Kew.

92
00:07:39,392 --> 00:07:41,052
Dannemann died in 1996.

93
00:07:49,262 --> 00:07:52,727
On 1 October, Hendrix was
buried in Renton, near Seattle,

94
00:07:53,047 --> 00:07:56,727
the city where he was born, after a
small service for family and friends.

95
00:07:57,444 --> 00:08:01,409
Miles Davis, Mitch Mitchell and Noel
Redding were among those who came.

96
00:08:01,989 --> 00:08:05,289
He had played his last full
concert twenty-five days earlier.

97
00:08:06,133 --> 00:08:08,353
A time, a cause, an open verdict.

98
00:08:08,913 --> 00:08:10,373
What do you remember of Jimi Hendrix?
